sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch06-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Potassium salts, like inorganic substances generally, raise surface tension and therefore concentrate at those parts of a cell surface where the tension is weak, so their localised accumulation (shown by Macallum's cobalt-nitrite method) indicates inequalities of surface tension.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Potassium salts concentrate where cell-surface tension is weak";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VI, “A Note on Adsorption”; topic “Potassium concentration and weakened tension”. Scope: plant and animal cells, solitary and in tissues"
}
